  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "V71s+xTzToSrf/kkVlAqcTIV2+U=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:43:24.602Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "`packages/common/drive-analytics/README.md` states that the processor \"implements the `IProcessor` interface and processes `InternalTransmitterUpdate` strands, which contain sequences of operations performed on document drives.\"\n\nAt v6.2.2-dev.74 that interface no longer exists. `packages/shared/processors/types.ts:54` declares `onOperations(operations: OperationWithContext[]): Promise<void>`, and a search of the tree finds **no `onStrands` and no `InternalTransmitterUpdate`** in package sources. This is the same drift the relational-DB processor spec shows, from the same v6 processor migration.\n\nWhat the README gets right and is worth trusting: the two processors, the `DriveOperations` counter, all four dimension path formats, the five action-type verbs, chunked processing with a default of 50, and the clear-and-rebuild-from-index-0 behaviour. None of those depend on the delivery interface.\n\nSo the reading rule for this file: **the dimensional model is current, the delivery API is not.** A developer copying its architecture section into a new processor will write a method the reactor never calls \u2014 a failure that is silent, because a processor with no matching method simply receives nothing.\n\n## Provenance\n- `packages/common/drive-analytics/README.md` \u2014 Architecture (IProcessor and InternalTransmitterUpdate strands)\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:43:24.602Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:43:24.602Z"
    }
  },
